            "contentHash": "d113bdb708cc8dfefe9fdf3400cb1b994fd5f50848990adf5cffb9d1c395ec81",
            "body": "The @c:\\dev\\ctrlyoke\\src\\ui\\webview-react\\components\\shared\\DryRunModal.tsx has two copy buttons (copy command and copy payload), and I'm trying to understand their usefulness. I definitely understand copy command, but the command itself often contains categorized prompt file references that are not written. So the usecase of \"copy command to run it myself\" is dependant on me having a simple prompt, plus it contains hooks and mcp file refs that wont be written. So what is the main benefit to giving them a copy command? Should we change it to coping a useful command, like Copy Runnable Command that strips any ctrlyoke specific hooks and tries to build a command that is actually paste-able? Also, the copy payload button was originally for copying the full raw prompt text that I could go paste into a running TUI, but copying the payload currently copies a massive json, which has a lot of useful data, I just don't know what we expect users to do with it. Originally the button was supposed to be for users to copy the expanded prompt text (with bare file refs still as just file refs, but inline content expanded fully in the xml), so users could build a prompt in ctrlyoke, and then copy the resulting to payload to a harness we don't necessarily support. The two use cases for these copy buttons I THOUGHT was supposed to be \"copy the command if you want to run it yourself\" and \"copy the payload if you want to paste the expanded content into a harness yourself\", but I think we failed in both of those cases. I'm sure the rational is somewhere in here @c:\\dev\\ctrlyoke\\dev\\backlog\\DRY_RUN_PREVIEW_PLAN.md"
          }
        ],
